<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Scaling generative AI models is bottlenecked by the scarcity of high-quality training data. The ease of synthesizing from a generative model suggests using (unverified) synthetic data to augment a limited corpus of real data for the purpose of fine-tuning in the hope of improving performance. Unfortunately, however, the resulting positive feedback loop leads to model autophagy disorder (MAD, aka model collapse) that results in a rapid degradation in sample quality and/or diversity. In this paper, we introduce Neon (for Negative Extrapolation frOm self-traiNing), a new learning method that turns the degradation from self-training into a powerful signal for self-improvement. Given a base model, Neon first fine-tunes it on its own self-synthesized data but then, counterintuitively, reverses its gradient updates to extrapolate away from the degraded weights. We prove that Neon works because typical inference samplers that favor high-probability regions create a predictable anti-alignment between the synthetic and real data population gradients, which negative extrapolation corrects to better align the model with the true data distribution. Neon is remarkably easy to implement via a simple post-hoc merge that requires no new real data, works effectively with as few as 1k synthetic samples, and typically uses less than 1% additional training compute. We demonstrate Neon’s universality across a range of architectures (diffusion, flow matching, autoregressive, and inductive moment matching models) and datasets (ImageNet, CIFAR-10, and FFHQ). In particular, on ImageNet 256x256, Neon elevates the xAR-L model to a new state-of-the-art FID of 1.02 with only 0.36% additional training compute. Code is available at <a href="https://github.com/SinaAlemohammad/Neon" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/SinaAlemohammad/Neon</a>
<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup>
          
          
        Corresponding author: <a href="mailto:sina.alemohammad@austin.utexas.edu" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">sina.alemohammad@austin.utexas.edu</a></span></span></span></p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="p1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_top">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:0.0pt;height:24.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"></span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<figure id="S0.F1" class="ltx_figure">
<div id="S0.F1.2" class="ltx_block ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:8.0pt;">
<div id="S0.F1.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:8.0pt;height:29.8pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="width:29.8pt;transform:translate(-11.4pt,-11.4pt) rotate(-90deg) ;">
<p id="S0.F1.2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S0.F1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">xAR-L</span></p>
</span></div>
<div id="S0.F1.2.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:8.0pt;height:66pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="width:66.0pt;transform:translate(-29.1pt,-29.1pt) rotate(-90deg) ;">
<p id="S0.F1.2.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S0.F1.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">xAR-L + Neon</span></p>
</span></div>
<div id="S0.F1.2.3" class="ltx_block ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:337.9pt;"><img src="2510.03597v1/images/figure1.png" id="S0.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:466/185;" width="466" height="185" alt="Refer to caption">
</div>
</div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span><span id="S0.F1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">
Good to great: Neon’s state-of-the-art performance on ImageNet-256.</span> Neon elevates a powerful baseline generative model (xAR-L, top row) to a new level of sharpness and realism (bottom row) with a simple post-hoc merge. This leap in quality, improving the Fréchet Inception Distance (FID) from 1.28 to a record-breaking 1.02, is accomplished with only 0.36% extra training compute.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Modern generative models for images have achieved remarkable photorealism through continuous advances in architectures, training methods, and scale. Diffusion models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, flow matching approaches <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lipman et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, autoregressive architectures <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ding et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, and few-step generators <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2025a</a>)</cite> now form the backbone of large-scale image generation systems. Despite these advances, the most reliable path to state-of-the-art performance remains scaling: ever more parameters, ever larger datasets, and ever increasing compute <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kaplan et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Henighan et al., 2020</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Important energy sustainability issues aside, this scaling paradigm faces a <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">fundamental bottleneck</span>: <span id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">high-quality training data</span>.
Curating diverse, rights-cleared image datasets is expensive and time-consuming, with diminishing returns as existing sources are exhausted <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Villalobos et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Muennighoff et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.
As the gap between model capacity and available training data widens, the field must explore alternative paths to model improvement that do not rely on ever-larger real datasets.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The ease of synthesizing data from generative models has inspired a range of model improvement approaches to augment a limited real data set.
At the simplistic end, one can fine-tune a model on its own generated outputs.
However, such naïve self-training has been shown to lead to “model autophagy disorder” (MAD) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alemohammad et al., 2024a</a>)</cite> or model collapse <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shumailov et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, where diversity and/or quality degrades.
At the complicated end, researchers have avoided collapse through sophisticated workarounds like external verifiers for synthetic data quality <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Feng et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, auxiliary discriminator networks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al., 2023a</a>)</cite>, negative guidance during inference <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alemohammad et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>, and likelihood-based discrimination between distributions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. While effective, these approaches add significant computational overhead, are restricted to specific architectures, or require complex iterative training.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Neon.</span> 
In this paper, <span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">we show that there is hidden promise in directly fine-tuning a model on its own generated data</span>.
Our key insight is that the degradation due to self-training is not random noise but rather a powerful signal that is anti-aligned with the real-data population gradient.
<span id="S1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Neon</span> (Negative Extrapolation from self-traiNing) exploits this anti-alignment through a simple parameter merge.
Given a base model with parameters <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> trained on real data, we first apply the naïve self-training approach: we generate synthetic samples and briefly fine-tune to obtain the parameters <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> that exhibit degraded performance.
Then, rather than using <math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> directly, we perform <span id="S1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">negative extrapolation</span>:</p>
<table id="S1.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{\text{Neon}}=\theta_{r}-w(\theta_{s}-\theta_{r})=(1+w)\theta_{r}-w\theta_{s},\qquad w&gt;0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>Neon</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{\text{Neon}}=\theta_{r}-w(\theta_{s}-\theta_{r})=(1+w)\theta_{r}-w\theta_{s},\qquad w&gt;0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> controls the extrapolation strength. The vector <math id="S1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{s}-\theta_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{s}-\theta_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to the synthetic gradient direction; because this direction is anti-aligned with the (infinite real data) population gradient, reversing it reduces the true data risk and redistributes probability mass to under-represented modes.
In the words of Martin Luther King, Jr.,
“Sometimes to move forward, we have to go backward.”</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Contributions.</span> 
[C1] We introduce <span id="S1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Neon</span>, a deceptively simple post-processing method that improves generative models by reversing their degradation on self-generated data (Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Neon: Negative Extrapolation from Self-Training ‣ Neon: Negative Extrapolation From Self-Training Improves Image Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).
In contrast to existing methods for synthetic data augmentation, Neon requires no additional real training data, no access to the original training data, no auxiliary models, no likelihood computation, and no inference modifications.
[C2] We prove rigorously that mode-seeking inference samplers create a predictable anti-alignment between the synthetic and population gradients that guarantees the effectiveness of negative extrapolation (Section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Why Neon Works ‣ 3 Neon: Negative Extrapolation from Self-Training ‣ Neon: Negative Extrapolation From Self-Training Improves Image Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>).
[C3] We demonstrate Neon’s universality across diffusion, flow matching (Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Diffusion and Flow Matching Models ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Neon: Negative Extrapolation From Self-Training Improves Image Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>), autoregressive (Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Autoregressive Models ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Neon: Negative Extrapolation From Self-Training Improves Image Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>), and few-step (Section <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 Few-Step Generators ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Neon: Negative Extrapolation From Self-Training Improves Image Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>) models on CIFAR-10, FFHQ, and ImageNet with <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>% additional compute and as few as 1k synthetic samples.
For example, on ImageNet-256, Neon elevates xAR-L from an FID of 1.28 to the state-of-the-art 1.02 using only 0.36% additional compute.
[C4] We show that Neon’s improvement mechanism operates through a precision-recall trade-off that redistributes probability mass from over- to under-represented modes (Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Diffusion and Flow Matching Models ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Neon: Negative Extrapolation From Self-Training Improves Image Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>).
[C5] We demonstrate that the Neon degradation signal is transferable, which enables synthetic data from one model architecture to improve another (Section <a href="#S4.SS4" title="4.4 Ablation studies ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Neon: Negative Extrapolation From Self-Training Improves Image Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Background</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Notation and definitions.</span> 
Let <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> be a training data set drawn from <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{data}}</annotation></semantics></math>. A training algorithm produces the generative model <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, whose output is a score, velocity, or logit depending on the model family. The training budget <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math> is the cumulative number of images seen (in millions): <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}=\text{(global steps)}\times\text{(global batch size)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>(global steps)</mtext><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mtext>(global batch size)</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}=\text{(global steps)}\times\text{(global batch size)}</annotation></semantics></math>. An inference routine <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math> with hyperparameters <math id="S2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> induces a sampling distribution <math id="S2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{\theta,\kappa}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{\theta,\kappa}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Denote the idealized distribution without inference-time modifications (e.g., guidance) by <math id="S2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}:=q_{\theta,\varnothing}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}:=q_{\theta,\varnothing}</annotation></semantics></math>. We use <math id="S2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{dist}(\cdot,\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>dist</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{dist}(\cdot,\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> for a generic divergence, <math id="S2.p1.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="|\cdot|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\cdot|</annotation></semantics></math> for set cardinality, and the shorthand</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\|x\|_{M}:=\|M^{1/2}x\|_{2},\quad \langle x,y\rangle_{M}:=x^{\top}My,\quad\|A\|_{\mathrm{op},M}:=\|M^{1/2}AM^{-1/2}\|_{\mathrm{op}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">∥</mo><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.0835em" rspace="0em">:=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mi>x</mi><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mi>M</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mi>M</mi><mi>y</mi><mo rspace="0.889em">,</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>A</mi><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">∥</mo><mrow><mi>op</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.0835em" rspace="0em">:=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>op</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|x\|_{M}:=\|M^{1/2}x\|_{2},\quad \langle x,y\rangle_{M}:=x^{\top}My,\quad\|A\|_{\mathrm{op},M}:=\|M^{1/2}AM^{-1/2}\|_{\mathrm{op}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">for any positive-definite matrix <math id="S2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.p1.m13" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\|\cdot\|_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em">∥</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\cdot\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\cdot,\cdot\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\cdot,\cdot\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.p1.m15" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\|\cdot\|_{\mathrm{op}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em">∥</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>op</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\cdot\|_{\mathrm{op}}</annotation></semantics></math> are the standard Euclidean norm, inner product, and operator norm.
Let k denote <math id="S2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>3</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Visual generative models.</span> 
Many image generators trace a path from noise to data via an affine interpolation
<math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}=\alpha(t)x_{0}+\sigma(t)\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}=\alpha(t)x_{0}+\sigma(t)\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>,
with <math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}\sim p_{\text{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}\sim p_{\text{data}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I)</annotation></semantics></math>, and boundary conditions <math id="S2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(0)=1,\ \sigma(0)=0,\ \alpha(1)=0,\ \sigma(1)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(0)=1,\ \sigma(0)=0,\ \alpha(1)=0,\ \sigma(1)=1</annotation></semantics></math>, inducing <math id="S2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}=p_{\text{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}=p_{\text{data}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1}=\mathcal{N}(0,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1}=\mathcal{N}(0,I)</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lipman et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Diffusion models</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2021</a>)</cite> train <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\theta}(x,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\theta}(x,t)</annotation></semantics></math> to approximate the score <math id="S2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{x}\log p_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{x}\log p_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> (or equivalently, predict noise). At inference, the learned score drives the reverse-time SDE or probability-flow ODE.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Flow matching</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lipman et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tong et al., 2024</a>)</cite> learns the conditional velocity
<math id="S2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v^{\star}(x_{0},\epsilon,t)=\alpha^{\prime}(t)x_{0}+\sigma^{\prime}(t)\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v^{\star}(x_{0},\epsilon,t)=\alpha^{\prime}(t)x_{0}+\sigma^{\prime}(t)\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>
by regressing <math id="S2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\theta}(x_{t},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\theta}(x_{t},t)</annotation></semantics></math> with squared error; sampling integrates <math id="S2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{x}_{t}=G_{\theta}(x_{t},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{x}_{t}=G_{\theta}(x_{t},t)</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Few-step generators</span> reduce sampling cost by collapsing many steps. Consistency models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2023</a>)</cite> predict <math id="S2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> directly from <math id="S2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{t},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{t},t)</annotation></semantics></math>; IMM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2025a</a>)</cite> learns direct transitions <math id="S2.p5.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x_{s}=G_{\theta}(x_{t},t\!\to\!s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{s}=G_{\theta}(x_{t},t\!\to\!s)</annotation></semantics></math> with moment-matching, enabling quality with <math id="S2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T{\approx}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T{\approx}1</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8</annotation></semantics></math> steps.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Autoregressive models</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tian et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ren et al., 2025</a>)</cite> factorize images into tokens <math id="S2.p6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="y_{1:N}=\mathcal{T}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{1:N}=\mathcal{T}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> and model
<math id="S2.p6.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p(y_{1:N})=\prod_{i=1}^{N}p(y_{\pi(i)}\mid y_{\pi(&lt;i)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>π</mi><mspace style="width:0.389em;" width="0.389em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(y_{1:N})=\prod_{i=1}^{N}p(y_{\pi(i)}\mid y_{\pi(&lt;i)})</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="S2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\theta}(y_{&lt;i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\theta}(y_{&lt;i})</annotation></semantics></math> outputs next-token logits trained via cross-entropy. The ordering <math id="S2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> and decoding choices (temperature, top-<math id="S2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>) form part of inference hyperparameters <math id="S2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Self-training and collapse.</span> 
When models iteratively train on their own synthetic outputs, they exhibit what has been termed MADness or model collapse: <math id="S2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[\text{dist}(p_{\text{data}},p_{\theta_{t}})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mtext>dist</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[\text{dist}(p_{\text{data}},p_{\theta_{t}})]</annotation></semantics></math> grows over time <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alemohammad et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shumailov et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dohmatob et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Pure self-training diverges, while mixing real and synthetic data converges to degraded equilibria <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bertrand et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gerstgrasser et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. While external signals beyond the training data can prevent collapse <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Feng et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alemohammad et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>, these methods require additional resources such as verifiers or fresh data.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Related work on synthetic data training.</span> 
Several recent methods successfully leverage synthetic data for model improvement, but require significant architectural constraints or computational overhead. Discriminator Guidance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al., 2023a</a>)</cite> trains a post-hoc discriminator on real versus generated samples across diffusion timesteps, using its gradients to correct the score function during sampling. While effective, it adds inference overhead and remains diffusion-specific. SIMS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alemohammad et al., 2024b</a>)</cite> employs self-generated data as negative guidance to steer diffusion trajectories away from degraded manifolds, but similarly requires inference-time modifications and is limited to diffusion models. Direct Discriminative Optimization (DDO) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2025</a>)</cite> reformulates likelihood-based models as implicit discriminators via log-likelihood ratios between target and reference models, enabling strong improvements for diffusion (via ELBO) and autoregressive models, but fundamentally cannot apply to likelihood-free architectures like flow matching <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lipman et al., 2023</a>)</cite> or inductive moment matching <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>. Self-Play Fine-Tuning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yuan et al., 2024</a>)</cite> iteratively pits models against earlier checkpoints, surpassing RLHF methods on human preference benchmarks but requiring multiple training rounds and substantial computational overhead. In contrast to these methods, Neon requires no auxiliary models, no inference modifications, no likelihood computations, and works across all architectures with a simple post-hoc parameter merge.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Neon: Negative Extrapolation from Self-Training</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When models train on synthetic samples produced by their inference procedure <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math> (what we call “self-training”), they predictably degrade.
Neon exploits this: by reversing the degradation direction, we can improve a model without additional real data.
Starting from a base generator <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\theta_{r}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\theta_{r}}</annotation></semantics></math> (typically trained on real data), we: (i) generate the synthetic dataset <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> once using test-time inference <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}(G_{\theta_{r}};\kappa)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>κ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}(G_{\theta_{r}};\kappa)</annotation></semantics></math>, (ii) briefly (e.g., using <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>% of the original training budget) fine-tune the generator on <math id="S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain the degraded <math id="S3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\theta_{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\theta_{s}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and (iii) negatively extrapolate via the parameter merge:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{\text{Neon}}:=\theta_{r}-w(\theta_{s}-\theta_{r})=(1+w)\theta_{r}-w\theta_{s}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>Neon</mtext></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{\text{Neon}}:=\theta_{r}-w(\theta_{s}-\theta_{r})=(1+w)\theta_{r}-w\theta_{s},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> controls the extrapolation strength. Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 3 Neon: Negative Extrapolation from Self-Training ‣ Neon: Negative Extrapolation From Self-Training Improves Image Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> provides the full details.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Neon: Negative Extrapolation from Self-Training</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">1:</span>
            
            
          Base model <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\theta_{r}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\theta_{r}}</annotation></semantics></math>, inference routine <math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math> with hyperparameters <math id="alg1.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">2:</span>
            
            
          <span id="alg1.l2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hyperparameters:</span> Synthetic dataset size <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{s}=|\mathcal{S}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{s}=|\mathcal{S}|</annotation></semantics></math>, extrapolation strength <math id="alg1.l2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>, training budget <math id="alg1.l2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">3:</span>
            
            
          <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}\leftarrow\{x_{i}\}_{i=1}^{n_{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}\leftarrow\{x_{i}\}_{i=1}^{n_{s}}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="alg1.l3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}\sim q_{\theta_{r},\kappa}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}\sim q_{\theta_{r},\kappa}</annotation></semantics></math> induced by <math id="alg1.l3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}(G_{\theta_{r}};\kappa)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>κ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}(G_{\theta_{r}};\kappa)</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l3.2" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><math id="alg1.l3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> sample using test-time inference
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">4:</span>
            
            
          <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\theta_{s}}\leftarrow\text{FineTune}(G_{\theta_{r}},\mathcal{S},\mathcal{B})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext>FineTune</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\theta_{s}}\leftarrow\text{FineTune}(G_{\theta_{r}},\mathcal{S},\mathcal{B})</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l4.2" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><math id="alg1.l4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> briefly fine-tune on synthetic data
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">5:</span>
            
            
          <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{\text{Neon}}\leftarrow(1+w)\theta_{r}-w\theta_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>Neon</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{\text{Neon}}\leftarrow(1+w)\theta_{r}-w\theta_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l5.2" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><math id="alg1.l5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> reverse the degradation
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">6:</span>
            
            
          Final generator <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\theta_{\text{Neon}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>Neon</mtext></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\theta_{\text{Neon}}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Why Neon Works</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Geometric intuition via a toy study.</span> 
To visualize why negative extrapolation from degradation succeeds, consider a 2D Gaussian example where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{data}}=\mathcal{N}(\mu_{\text{true}},\Sigma_{\text{true}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mtext>true</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mtext>true</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{data}}=\mathcal{N}(\mu_{\text{true}},\Sigma_{\text{true}})</annotation></semantics></math>. We train a base model <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\theta_{r}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\theta_{r}}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>k real samples and then define two directions in parameter space:
the <em id="S3.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">degradation direction</em> from fine-tuning the base model on <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>5</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{5}</annotation></semantics></math> synthetic samples from <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\theta_{r}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\theta_{r}}</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\theta_{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\theta_{s}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and an oracle <em id="S3.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">improvement direction</em> from fine-tuning on <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>k real samples (the original <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>k real data points plus <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>k new ones) to obtain <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\theta_{o}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\theta_{o}}</annotation></semantics></math>. We evaluate models in the 2D span of these directions:</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta(w_{s},w_{o})\;=\;\theta_{r}\;+\;w_{s}\underbrace{(\theta_{r}-\theta_{s})}_{\text{$-$ degradation direction (Neon)}}\;+\;w_{o}\underbrace{(\theta_{o}-\theta_{r})}_{\text{oracle improvement direction}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.502em">+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">−</mo><mtext> degradation direction (Neon)</mtext></mrow></munder></mrow><mo rspace="0.502em">+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>oracle improvement direction</mtext></munder></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta(w_{s},w_{o})\;=\;\theta_{r}\;+\;w_{s}\underbrace{(\theta_{r}-\theta_{s})}_{\text{$-$ degradation direction (Neon)}}\;+\;w_{o}\underbrace{(\theta_{o}-\theta_{r})}_{\text{oracle improvement direction}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> controls the amount of negative extrapolation (Neon) and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{o}</annotation></semantics></math> adds real-data improvement (oracle baseline).
Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3.1 Why Neon Works ‣ 3 Neon: Negative Extrapolation from Self-Training ‣ Neon: Negative Extrapolation From Self-Training Improves Image Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> visualizes our key finding: moving backwards along the Neon direction alone (<math id="S3.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{o}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{o}=0</annotation></semantics></math>) yields substantial improvement, indicating that the opposite of degradation direction and additional real-data improvement direction both point towards a better approximation of the true data distribution.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.03597v1/images/heatmap_neon.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:192/137;" width="192" height="137" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span><span id="S3.F2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Neon’s key idea: synthetic degradation and real-data improvement point in opposite directions.</span> This toy 2D Gaussian example plots as a heat map the log Wasserstein distance to the true data distribution <math id="S3.F2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{data}}</annotation></semantics></math> from the generative model <math id="S3.F2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\theta(w_{s},w_{o})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\theta(w_{s},w_{o})}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We see that updating the model’s parameters in the reverse of the direction they would be updated by fine-tuning on self-synthesized data (increasing <math id="S3.F2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>) achieves similar improvements to fine-tuning the base model with <math id="S3.F2.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="4\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4\times</annotation></semantics></math> more real data (increasing <math id="S3.F2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{o}</annotation></semantics></math>).</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theoretical analysis.</span> 
We now formalize the intuition provided by the toy example. We prove that typical inference samplers cause the synthetic and real data gradients to point in opposite directions, enabling negative extrapolation to reduce the true data risk.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Set-up.</span> 
Let <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{\theta}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{\theta}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> be differentiable loss function and <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{data}}(\theta):=\mathbb{E}_{p_{\text{data}}}[\ell_{\theta}(X)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{data}}(\theta):=\mathbb{E}_{p_{\text{data}}}[\ell_{\theta}(X)]</annotation></semantics></math> the corresponding risk.
Let <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{*}\in\arg\min_{\theta}\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{data}}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>min</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{*}\in\arg\min_{\theta}\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{data}}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> and write <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{r}=\theta^{*}+\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{r}=\theta^{*}+\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> with
<math id="S3.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\varepsilon\|_{H_{d}}^{2}=\varepsilon^{\top}H_{d}\,\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ε</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\varepsilon\|_{H_{d}}^{2}=\varepsilon^{\top}H_{d}\,\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{\theta_{r},\kappa}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{\theta_{r},\kappa}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the fixed sampler constructed once at <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>. Define</p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex2X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex2X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\phi_{\theta}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\phi_{\theta}(x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex2X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle:=\nabla_{\theta}\ell_{\theta}(x)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle:=\nabla_{\theta}\ell_{\theta}(x),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex2X.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle H_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle H_{d}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex2X.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle:=\nabla^{2}\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{data}}(\theta^{*})=\mathbb{E}_{p_{\text{data}}}\!\big[\partial_{\theta}\phi_{\theta}(X)\big]_{\theta=\theta^{*}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>data</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle:=\nabla^{2}\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{data}}(\theta^{*})=\mathbb{E}_{p_{\text{data}}}\!\big[\partial_{\theta}\phi_{\theta}(X)\big]_{\theta=\theta^{*}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex2Xa"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex2Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{syn}}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>syn</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{syn}}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex2Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle:=\mathbb{E}_{x\sim q_{\theta_{r},\kappa}}\![\ell_{\theta}(x)]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle:=\mathbb{E}_{x\sim q_{\theta_{r},\kappa}}\![\ell_{\theta}(x)],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex2Xa.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle r_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle r_{d}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex2Xa.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle:=\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{data}}(\theta)\big|_{\theta_{r}},\qquad r_{s}:=\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{syn}}(\theta)\big|_{\theta_{r}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo lspace="0em">=</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>data</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo fence="false" maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="true">|</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></msub><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>r</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>syn</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo fence="false" maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle:=\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{data}}(\theta)\big|_{\theta_{r}},\qquad r_{s}:=\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{syn}}(\theta)\big|_{\theta_{r}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\succ 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>≻</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\succ 0</annotation></semantics></math> be a preconditioner and set <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K:=H_{d}^{1/2}PH_{d}^{1/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K:=H_{d}^{1/2}PH_{d}^{1/2}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="mI\preceq K\preceq MI" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo>⪯</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>⪯</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">mI\preceq K\preceq MI</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We say the synthetic and real data gradients are <span id="S3.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">anti-aligned</span> at <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> if
their preconditioned inner product is negative</p>
<table id="S3.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s:=\langle r_{d},\,P\,r_{s}\rangle&lt;0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s:=\langle r_{d},\,P\,r_{s}\rangle&lt;0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Neon improves under anti-alignment.</span> 
Short synthetic fine-tuning yields <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{s}=\theta_{r}-\alpha\,P\,r_{s}+O(\alpha^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{s}=\theta_{r}-\alpha\,P\,r_{s}+O(\alpha^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, which Neon reverses: <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{\text{Neon}}=\theta_{r}+w\alpha\,Pr_{s}+O(w\alpha^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>Neon</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{\text{Neon}}=\theta_{r}+w\alpha\,Pr_{s}+O(w\alpha^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>.
A Taylor expansion of the risk yields</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{data}}(\theta_{\text{Neon}})=\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{data}}(\theta_{r})+w\alpha\,s+\frac{(w\alpha)^{2}}{2}\,r_{s}^{\top}P^{\top}\nabla^{2}\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{data}}(\theta_{r})\,P\,r_{s}+O\!\big((w\alpha)^{3}\big)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>Neon</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msup><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>data</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.621em;" width="0.621em"><mi>O</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{data}}(\theta_{\text{Neon}})=\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{data}}(\theta_{r})+w\alpha\,s+\frac{(w\alpha)^{2}}{2}\,r_{s}^{\top}P^{\top}\nabla^{2}\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{data}}(\theta_{r})\,P\,r_{s}+O\!\big((w\alpha)^{3}\big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.2" class="ltx_p">When <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, the negative linear term dominates for small <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, ensuring that <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{data}}(\theta_{\text{Neon}})&lt;\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{data}}(\theta_{r})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>Neon</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{data}}(\theta_{\text{Neon}})&lt;\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{data}}(\theta_{r})</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{\text{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{\text{data}}</annotation></semantics></math> is locally convex at <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla^{2}\mathcal{R}_{\text{data}}(\theta_{r})\succeq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mo>∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⪰</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla^{2}\mathcal{R}_{\text{data}}(\theta_{r})\succeq 0</annotation></semantics></math>), the optimal <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="w^{*}=-s/(\alpha z)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{*}=-s/(\alpha z)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z:=r_{s}^{\top}P^{\top}\nabla^{2}\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{data}}(\theta_{r})Pr_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msup><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>data</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z:=r_{s}^{\top}P^{\top}\nabla^{2}\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{data}}(\theta_{r})Pr_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>.<span id="footnote1a" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
            Local convexity is sufficient but not necessary. The result holds under the weaker condition of directional smoothness along the step direction <math id="footnote1a.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=Pr_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=Pr_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>. See Appendix <a href="#A2.SS2" title="B.2 Neon improves under anti-alignment ‣ Appendix B Proofs and Detailed Explanations ‣ Neon: Negative Extrapolation From Self-Training Improves Image Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2</span></a> for details.</span></span></span> See Appendix <a href="#A2.SS2" title="B.2 Neon improves under anti-alignment ‣ Appendix B Proofs and Detailed Explanations ‣ Neon: Negative Extrapolation From Self-Training Improves Image Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2</span></a> for the proof.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sampler-induced anti-alignment.</span> 
Let</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\;:=\;\mathbb{E}_{q_{\theta_{r},\kappa}}\!\big[\phi_{\theta^{*}}(X)\big],\quad\Delta\;:=\;\mathbb{E}_{q_{\theta_{r},\kappa}}\!\big[J_{\theta^{*}}(X)\big]\;-\;\mathbb{E}_{p_{\text{data}}}\!\big[J_{\theta^{*}}(X)\big],\quad J_{\theta^{*}}(x):=\partial_{\theta}\phi_{\theta}(x)\big|_{\theta^{*}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.280em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.502em">−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><msub><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\;:=\;\mathbb{E}_{q_{\theta_{r},\kappa}}\!\big[\phi_{\theta^{*}}(X)\big],\quad\Delta\;:=\;\mathbb{E}_{q_{\theta_{r},\kappa}}\!\big[J_{\theta^{*}}(X)\big]\;-\;\mathbb{E}_{p_{\text{data}}}\!\big[J_{\theta^{*}}(X)\big],\quad J_{\theta^{*}}(x):=\partial_{\theta}\phi_{\theta}(x)\big|_{\theta^{*}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p6.2" class="ltx_p">and measure their sizes in the <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>–geometry by</p>
<table id="S3.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{0}:=\|b\|_{H_{d}^{-1}},\qquad\eta_{1}:=\|\Delta\|_{\mathrm{op},\,H_{d}^{-1}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>:=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></msub></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>:=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>op</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{0}:=\|b\|_{H_{d}^{-1}},\qquad\eta_{1}:=\|\Delta\|_{\mathrm{op},\,H_{d}^{-1}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p6.3" class="ltx_p">Define the angle between the model error <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> and the sampler bias <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> in the <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>–geometry by</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cos\varphi\;:=\;\frac{\langle\varepsilon,\;H_{d}^{-1}b\rangle_{H_{d}}}{\|\varepsilon\|_{H_{d}}\,\|H_{d}^{-1}b\|_{H_{d}}}\in[-1,1]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>φ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">:=</mo><mfrac><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></msub><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cos\varphi\;:=\;\frac{\langle\varepsilon,\;H_{d}^{-1}b\rangle_{H_{d}}}{\|\varepsilon\|_{H_{d}}\,\|H_{d}^{-1}b\|_{H_{d}}}\in[-1,1].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p6.4" class="ltx_p">Intuitively, <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cos\varphi&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>φ</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cos\varphi&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math> means that the sampler’s bias points is in a direction opposing the current error, favoring anti-alignment.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Anti-alignment under inference mismatch)<span id="Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K:=H_{d}^{1/2}PH_{d}^{1/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K:=H_{d}^{1/2}PH_{d}^{1/2}</annotation></semantics></math> with spectral bounds <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="mI\preceq K\preceq MI" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo>⪯</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>⪯</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">mI\preceq K\preceq MI</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then the alignment <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=\langle r_{d},\,Pr_{s}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=\langle r_{d},\,Pr_{s}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> obeys</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\;\leq\;M(1+\eta_{1})\,\|\varepsilon\|_{H_{d}}^{2}\;-\;m\,\eta_{0}\,\|\varepsilon\|_{H_{d}}\,[-\cos\varphi]_{+}\;+\;O(\|\varepsilon\|_{H_{d}}^{3})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0.502em">−</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>φ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.502em">+</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\;\leq\;M(1+\eta_{1})\,\|\varepsilon\|_{H_{d}}^{2}\;-\;m\,\eta_{0}\,\|\varepsilon\|_{H_{d}}\,[-\cos\varphi]_{+}\;+\;O(\|\varepsilon\|_{H_{d}}^{3}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consequently, a <em id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">sufficient</em> condition for <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math> is that the leading two terms on the right-hand side be negative.
In particular, for <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cos\varphi&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>φ</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cos\varphi&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and sufficiently small <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\varepsilon\|_{H_{d}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\varepsilon\|_{H_{d}}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boxed{\ \|\varepsilon\|_{H_{d}}\;&lt;\;\frac{m\,\eta_{0}}{M(1+\eta_{1})}\,(-\cos\varphi)\ }\ \Longrightarrow\ s&lt;0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><menclose notation="box"><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">&lt;</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>φ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></menclose><mo lspace="0.778em" rspace="0.778em" stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boxed{\ \|\varepsilon\|_{H_{d}}\;&lt;\;\frac{m\,\eta_{0}}{M(1+\eta_{1})}\,(-\cos\varphi)\ }\ \Longrightarrow\ s&lt;0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">See Appendices <a href="#A2.SS2" title="B.2 Neon improves under anti-alignment ‣ Appendix B Proofs and Detailed Explanations ‣ Neon: Negative Extrapolation From Self-Training Improves Image Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2</span></a>–<a href="#A2.SS3" title="B.3 An upper bound on 𝑠 and sufficient conditions for anti-alignment ‣ Appendix B Proofs and Detailed Explanations ‣ Neon: Negative Extrapolation From Self-Training Improves Image Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.3</span></a> for the proof.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Mode-seeking samplers induce <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span> 
The inference routines of many of today’s generative models can be written as a monotone reweighting of the reference model</p>
<table id="S3.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(x)\ \propto\ f\!\big(\log p_{\theta_{r}}(x)\big)\,p_{\theta_{r}}(x),\quad\text{with $f$ nondecreasing and not a.e.\ constant.}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">∝</mo><mrow><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.427em;" width="0.427em"><mi>f</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mtext>with </mtext><mi>f</mi><mtext> nondecreasing and not a.e. constant.</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(x)\ \propto\ f\!\big(\log p_{\theta_{r}}(x)\big)\,p_{\theta_{r}}(x),\quad\text{with $f$ nondecreasing and not a.e.\ constant.}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p8.2" class="ltx_p">Such <span id="S3.SS1.p8.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">mode-seeking</span> samplers emphasize high-density regions and (to first order near <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>) produce an <span id="S3.SS1.p8.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">obtuse</span> angle with <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cos\varphi&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>φ</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cos\varphi&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S3.E6" title="In 3.1 Why Neon Works ‣ 3 Neon: Negative Extrapolation from Self-Training ‣ Neon: Negative Extrapolation From Self-Training Improves Image Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>).
Combining this with Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1 (Anti-alignment under inference mismatch). ‣ 3.1 Why Neon Works ‣ 3 Neon: Negative Extrapolation from Self-Training ‣ Neon: Negative Extrapolation From Self-Training Improves Image Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> yields a transparent sufficient condition for <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math> near
strong base models (i.e small <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\varepsilon\|_{H_{d}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\varepsilon\|_{H_{d}}</annotation></semantics></math>); hence, negative extrapolation (<math id="S3.SS1.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> in
(<a href="#S3.E2" title="In 3 Neon: Negative Extrapolation from Self-Training ‣ Neon: Negative Extrapolation From Self-Training Improves Image Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>)
reduces the real-data risk <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{\text{data}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{\text{data}}.</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Some concrete instances: (i) AR: temperature <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math> and top-<math id="S3.SS1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>/<math id="S3.SS1.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> truncation yield nondecreasing reweighting of <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log p_{\theta_{r}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log p_{\theta_{r}}</annotation></semantics></math>; see Appendix <a href="#A2.SS4" title="B.4 Acute-angle conditions that imply 𝑠&lt;0 (AR models) ‣ Appendix B Proofs and Detailed Explanations ‣ Neon: Negative Extrapolation From Self-Training Improves Image Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.4</span></a> for the proof for AR models. (ii) Diffusion/flow: finite-step ODE solvers (including classifier-free guidance (CFG) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho &amp; Salimans, 2022</a>)</cite>) induce monotone terminal reweighting to first order in step size; see Appendix <a href="#A2.SS5" title="B.5 Acute-angle conditions that imply 𝑠&lt;0 (diffusion &amp; flow) ‣ Appendix B Proofs and Detailed Explanations ‣ Neon: Negative Extrapolation From Self-Training Improves Image Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.5</span></a> for the proof for diffusion models.<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
              
              
              
            For the proof of finite-step
ODE solvers being mode-seeking, we assume curvature–density coupling: contraction <math id="footnote2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[\sum_{k}\|\nabla_{x}f(X_{t_{k}},t_{k})\|_{\mathrm{Fr}}^{2}|X_{0}=x_{0}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>Fr</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[\sum_{k}\|\nabla_{x}f(X_{t_{k}},t_{k})\|_{\mathrm{Fr}}^{2}|X_{0}=x_{0}]</annotation></semantics></math> increases with <math id="footnote2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log p_{\theta_{r}}(x_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log p_{\theta_{r}}(x_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">When Neon fails.</span> 
Neon’s success requires <math id="S3.SS1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math> (negative gradient alignment).
If the sampler is not mode-seeking but rather diversity-seeking — meaning that it upweights low-probability regions via <math id="S3.SS1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(x)\propto f(\log p_{\theta_{r}}(x))p_{\theta_{r}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(x)\propto f(\log p_{\theta_{r}}(x))p_{\theta_{r}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS1.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> nonincreasing — then
our theory shows that <math id="S3.SS1.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> near good models (small <math id="S3.SS1.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\varepsilon\|_{H_{d}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\varepsilon\|_{H_{d}}</annotation></semantics></math>).
In this case, standard self-training (moving toward <math id="S3.SS1.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>, equivalent to negative <math id="S3.SS1.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>) would actually improve the model, while Neon’s prescription (positive <math id="S3.SS1.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>) would harm it.
Diversity-seeking samplers are rare in practice: they require temperature <math id="S3.SS1.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> for AR models or specialized samplers that decrease contraction near modes for diffusion models, both of which are rare design choices.
See Appendix <a href="#A2.SS7" title="B.7 When self-training helps ‣ Appendix B Proofs and Detailed Explanations ‣ Neon: Negative Extrapolation From Self-Training Improves Image Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.7</span></a> for more details.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p11" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Finite <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{S}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{S}|</annotation></semantics></math> effects.</span>  Our analysis assumes that the population synthetic gradients <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{s}(\theta_{r})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{s}(\theta_{r})</annotation></semantics></math>, but in practice we use finite <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> with brief fine-tuning from <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>. For checkpoint <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> after <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> steps with step size <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, the displacement <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{T}:=(\theta_{s}-\theta_{r})/(\alpha T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{T}:=(\theta_{s}-\theta_{r})/(\alpha T)</annotation></semantics></math> concentrates on <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-Pr_{s}^{(\mathcal{S})}(\theta_{r})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-Pr_{s}^{(\mathcal{S})}(\theta_{r})</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficiently large while <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha T</annotation></semantics></math> remains small, yielding stable, low-variance Neon directions despite limited <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{S}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{S}|</annotation></semantics></math>. This produces a U-shaped performance in <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{S}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{S}|</annotation></semantics></math>: very small sets are variance-limited, very large sets amplify curvature effects (inflating the quadratic term in our Taylor expansion), while moderate sizes optimally balance these competing factors.
See Appendix <a href="#A2.SS8" title="B.8 Notes on finite synthetic set and effect of short fine-tuning ‣ Appendix B Proofs and Detailed Explanations ‣ Neon: Negative Extrapolation From Self-Training Improves Image Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.8</span></a> for formal bounds and parameter selection guidance.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate Neon across four model families — diffusion (EDM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karras et al., 2022</a>)</cite>), flow matching <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tong et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tong et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, autoregressive (VAR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tian et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, xAR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ren et al., 2025</a>)</cite>), and few-step (IMM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>) — on ImageNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deng et al., 2009</a>)</cite>, CIFAR-10 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Krizhevsky &amp; Hinton, 2009</a>)</cite>, and FFHQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karras et al., 2019</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For each model, starting from a public checkpoint <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\theta_{r}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\theta_{r}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we generate synthetic datasets <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> using the FID-optimal inference settings <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> from each paper. We fine-tune on <math id="S4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> with the original training recipe at reduced learning rate (see Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Experiments Details ‣ Neon: Negative Extrapolation From Self-Training Improves Image Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a> for details). We report FID as our primary metric using 10k/50k samples for hyperparameter search/final evaluation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Heusel et al., 2017</a>)</cite>, with Precision/Recall <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kynkäänniemi et al., 2019</a>)</cite> at <math id="S4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=5</annotation></semantics></math> nearest neighbors. For a comprehensive comparison of Neon against state-of-the-art generative models across all benchmarks, please see Table <a href="#A1.T1" title="Table A.1 ‣ Appendix A State of the art comparison ‣ Neon: Negative Extrapolation From Self-Training Improves Image Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
