<p id="S4.F6.5.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">Asymptotic Recall-Precision</p><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.03597v1/figure6_4.svg" id="S4.F6.g4" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:132/118;" width="132" height="118"></object>
</div></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span><span id="S4.F6.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">
Optimal precision-recall trade-offs for VAR-d16 as a function of <math id="S4.F6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.F6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>
Left: Heatmaps for FID, precision, and recall on ImageNet-256 (<math id="S4.F6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{S}|{=}750" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>750</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{S}|{=}750</annotation></semantics></math>k, <math id="S4.F6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}{=}1.25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.25</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}{=}1.25</annotation></semantics></math>Mi) from a grid search over <math id="S4.F6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.F6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>.
The star marks the best FID <math id="S4.F6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(w^{*}{\approx}1.0,\gamma^{*}{\approx}2.7)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>≈</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>≈</mo><mn>2.7</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(w^{*}{\approx}1.0,\gamma^{*}{\approx}2.7)</annotation></semantics></math> achieving FID 2.01, unreachable by either parameter alone.
Right: Asymptotic precision-recall curves showing expanded behavioral range through joint tuning.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Autoregressive Models</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate Neon’s impact on xAR-B and xAR-L <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ren et al., 2025</a>)</cite> (ImageNet-256), VAR-d16 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tian et al., 2024</a>)</cite> (ImageNet-256), and VAR-d30 (ImageNet-512).
Both model families use CFG, with VAR adding top-<math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>/top-<math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> sampling; these are mode-seeking samplers, and so our theory predicts Neon benefits.
At evaluation, we jointly optimize both the merge weight <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> and CFG scale <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>.
Co-optimization is crucial to reaching the best FID: <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> increases recall at precision’s expense, while <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> does the opposite.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Results.</span> 
Figure <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 4.1 Diffusion and Flow Matching Models ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Neon: Negative Extrapolation From Self-Training Improves Image Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> depicts the best FID after <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\gamma,w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\gamma,w)</annotation></semantics></math> grid search versus fine-tuning budget <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math>, testing up to <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{S}|=750" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>750</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{S}|=750</annotation></semantics></math>k synthetic samples.
The xAR family FID improves monotonically: xAR-B from <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.72" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.72</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.72</annotation></semantics></math> to <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1.31</span> (<math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="750" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>750</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">750</annotation></semantics></math>k synthetic samples, <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.41\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.41</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.41\%</annotation></semantics></math> additional compute); xAR-L from <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.28" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.28</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.28</annotation></semantics></math> to the state-of-the-art FID <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1.02</span> (<math id="S4.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="750" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>750</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">750</annotation></semantics></math>k samples, <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.36\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.36</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.36\%</annotation></semantics></math> additional compute), surpassing UCGM’s <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.06" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.06</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.06</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Even with just <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>k samples, the xAR models achieve near-optimal performance (xAR-L: <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.05</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.05</annotation></semantics></math>, xAR-B: <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.36" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.36</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.36</annotation></semantics></math>), indicating that the degradation direction stabilizes quickly and requires minimal synthetic data to identify. VAR-d16 improves from <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3.30</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.30</annotation></semantics></math> to <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2.01</span> (<math id="S4.SS2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="750" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>750</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">750</annotation></semantics></math>k samples, <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.64\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.64</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.64\%</annotation></semantics></math> additional compute) but requires larger synthetic datasets—performance degrades with <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{S}|&lt;90" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>90</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{S}|&lt;90</annotation></semantics></math>k. VAR-d30 achieves its best FID of <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1.69</span> with just <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="90" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>90</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">90</annotation></semantics></math>k samples; adding more synthetic data provides no further meaningful improvement, suggesting the model has reached its capacity for Neon-based enhancement at this checkpoint.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 4.1 Diffusion and Flow Matching Models ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Neon: Negative Extrapolation From Self-Training Improves Image Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> visualizes the <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(w,\gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(w,\gamma)</annotation></semantics></math> interaction for VAR-d16. The FID landscape’s diagonal valley with optimum <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(w^{*}{\approx}1.0,\gamma^{*}{\approx}2.7)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>≈</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>≈</mo><mn>2.7</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(w^{*}{\approx}1.0,\gamma^{*}{\approx}2.7)</annotation></semantics></math> yields FID <span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2.01</span>. Independent optimization (<math id="S4.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma{=}1.25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.25</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma{=}1.25</annotation></semantics></math>) yields FID <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3.01</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.01</annotation></semantics></math> — far worse.
Joint tuning enables precision-recall trade-offs unreachable by either parameter alone: at the optimum, precision drops to <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sim}0.87" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mn>0.87</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sim}0.87</annotation></semantics></math> while recall rises to <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sim}0.63" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mn>0.63</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sim}0.63</annotation></semantics></math>. The rightmost panel reveals the asymptotic behavior: as <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> increases, the models converge to high precision (<math id="S4.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;0.95" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0.95</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;0.95</annotation></semantics></math>) but severely degraded recall (<math id="S4.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;0.45" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.45</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;0.45</annotation></semantics></math>), leading to mode collapse. Higher <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> values provide partial protection — at <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w=2</annotation></semantics></math>, the low-recall limit rises to <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sim}0.55" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mn>0.55</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sim}0.55</annotation></semantics></math> vs. <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sim}0.40" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mn>0.40</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sim}0.40</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w=0</annotation></semantics></math>, demonstrating how negative extrapolation counteracts CFG’s mode-seeking tendency even at extreme guidance scales.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Few-Step Generators</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We investigate Neon paired with Inductive Moment Matching (IMM) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2025a</a>)</cite> on ImageNet-256. We generated <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> using <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T{=}8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T{=}8</annotation></semantics></math> steps with CFG scale <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma{=}1.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma{=}1.5</annotation></semantics></math>. At evaluation, we tested the models across inference steps <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T{\in}\{1,2,4,8\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T{\in}\{1,2,4,8\}</annotation></semantics></math> and jointly searched over <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(w,\gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(w,\gamma)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Results.</span> 
Figure <a href="#S4.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 4.3 Few-Step Generators ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Neon: Negative Extrapolation From Self-Training Improves Image Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> plots the FID vs. the fine-tuning budget <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Neon delivers dramatic improvements across all step counts with minimal overhead relative to IMM’s 40,960Mi training budget. Performance scales inversely with the number of inference steps.
Neon improves <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T{=}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T{=}1</annotation></semantics></math> (single-step) inference to an FID of <span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">6.67</span>.
<math id="S4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T{=}2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T{=}2</annotation></semantics></math> reaches <span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2.89</span>; <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T{=}4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T{=}4</annotation></semantics></math> reaches <span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1.69</span>; and <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T{=}8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T{=}8</annotation></semantics></math> reaches <span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1.46</span>.
Remarkably, 4-step inference nearly matches base model with 8-step quality (1.69 vs. 1.98), effectively halving the inference cost.
Unlike IMM’s tens of thousands of million-image steps, Neon achieves optimal performance within <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>Mi in all experiments for different <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{S}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{S}|</annotation></semantics></math>, demonstrating rapid degradation direction stabilization for few-step models. The 30k sample sweet spot across all <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> suggests that few-step generators are particularly well-suited for Neon, as their training already distills multi-step dynamics into compact transitions, making the synthetic degradation signal especially informative.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F7" class="ltx_figure"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.F7.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" height="161.62" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 568.64 161.62" width="568.64"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,161.62) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(46.57,0) translate(0,39.89)"><g transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -46.57 -39.88)"><g class="ltx_nestedsvg" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 0) translate(46.57,0) translate(0,47.35)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#BFBFBF;--ltx-fill-color:#BFBFBF;--ltx-fg-color:#BFBFBF;" color="#BFBFBF" fill="#BFBFBF" stroke="#BFBFBF" stroke-width="0.4pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 0 -7.46 L 0 82.07 M 37.91 -7.46 L 37.91 82.07 M 75.82 -7.46 L 75.82 82.07 M 113.73 -7.46 L 113.73 82.07"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#BFBFBF;--ltx-fill-color:#BFBFBF;--ltx-fg-color:#BFBFBF;" color="#BFBFBF" fill="#BFBFBF" stroke="#BFBFBF" stroke-width="0.4pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 0 20.63 L 113.73 20.63 M 0 52.16 L 113.73 52.16"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#808080;--ltx-fill-color:#808080;--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" color="#808080" fill="#808080" stroke="#808080" stroke-width="0.2pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 0 -7.46 L 0 -1.56 M 37.91 -7.46 L 37.91 -1.56 M 75.82 -7.46 L 75.82 -1.56 M 113.73 -7.46 L 113.73 -1.56"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#808080;--ltx-fill-color:#808080;--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" color="#808080" fill="#808080" stroke="#808080" stroke-width="0.2pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 0 20.63 L 5.91 20.63 M 0 52.16 L 5.91 52.16"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="1.2pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 0 -7.46 L 113.73 -7.46"></path><path style="fill:none" d="M 0 -7.46 L 0 82.07"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="m